# TypeScript guide

Follow current TypeScript (5.x+) practice. Prefer the type checker over runtime cleverness.

## Types first

- Prefer `unknown` over `any`; narrow before use.
- Use `interface` for object shapes that may extend; use `type` for unions, intersections, mapped/conditional types.
- Prefer `readonly` / `Readonly<>` for data that must not mutate at the type level.
- Prefer `as const` for literal tuples/objects that should stay narrow.
- Prefer `satisfies` when a value must match a contract without widening its inferred type.
- Prefer `const` type parameters on generic helpers that should preserve literals.
- Prefer discriminated unions over optional flag soup.
- Prefer exact return types at module boundaries; avoid leaking huge inferred object types from public APIs.

## Modules & imports

- Use ES modules only (`import` / `export`).
- Prefer named exports; default exports only when a framework requires them.
- Import types with `import type` when the value is erased.
- Do not use `require` or namespace imports except for established third-party prefixes (e.g. `THREE` if needed).

## Naming (identifiers)

- `UpperCamelCase` — classes, types, interfaces, enums.
- `lowerCamelCase` — variables, functions, methods, parameters.
- `CONSTANT_CASE` — true constants and enum members.
- Do not prefix interfaces with `I`. Do not use leading/trailing `_` on identifiers.
- Names carry meaning; do not encode the type in the name (`userList: User[]` → `users`).

## Nullability & errors

- Prefer explicit `| null` / `| undefined` over lying types.
- Prefer early returns over deep nesting.
- When catching, type as `unknown` and narrow; rethrow with `cause` when wrapping.
- Do not swallow errors at trust boundaries (loaders, plugin bootstrap, spatial edits).

## Classes vs functions

- Prefer plain functions and data for pure logic.
- Use classes when there is clear identity + lifecycle state (engines, controllers, registries).
- Prefer `readonly` fields set in the constructor over reassignment.

## Project fit

- Keep public types at feature boundaries (`*.types.ts` / package indexes).
- Scenario/site data stays declarative and typed; do not push simulation into config files.
- Grid / spatial code stays Three-free and DOM-free.
- Match `docs/general/CODE_CONVENTIONS.md` and `FILE_STRUCTURES.md` for file roles and layout.
